nallocated financial reserves level - end","form":"RS","row":"916","col":"3"}},{"label":"Other earmarked reserves","code":"RS_resoth_end_cy","by_year":{"2017-18":3895.0,"2018-19":3792.0,"2019-20":2851.0,"2020-21":7764.0,"2021-22":9802.0,"2022-23":10347.0,"2023-24":11768.0,"2024-25":12971.0},"prov":{"description":"Other earmarked financial reserves level - end","form":"RS","row":"915","col":"3"}}]},{"id":"council_tax_ratio","label":"Council tax requirement","unit":"pct","ratio":{"2017-18":61.69,"2018-19":63.63,"2019-20":66.17,"2020-21":66.72,"2021-22":59.27,"2022-23":69.14,"2023-24":64.63,"2024-25":60.29},"parts":[{"label":"Council tax requirement","code":"RS_ctrtot_net_exp","by_year":{"2017-18":5991.0,"2018-19":6210.0,"2019-20":6463.0,"2020-21":6674.0,"2021-22":6881.0,"2022-23":7192.0,"2023-24":7427.0,"2024-25":7924.0},"prov":{"description":"Financing - COUNCIL TAX REQUIREMENT (TOTAL OF LINES 805 TO 885)","form":"RS","row":"890","col":"1"}},{"label":"Net revenue expenditure","code":"RS_netrevexp_net_exp","by_year":{"2017-18":9712.0,"2018-19":9759.0,"2019-20":9768.0,"2020-21":10003.0,"2021-22":11609.0,"2022-23":10402.0,"2023-24":11492.0,"2024-25":13144.0},"prov":{"description":"NET current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":92633,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":1796000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":19614,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":22766,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2384.66,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":230.89,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"High Peak council kept borrowing under control in 2025/26, using its own cash to cut debt costs.","lead":"High Peak council spent \u00a317.1m on capital projects in the year to March 2026, of which \u00a312.1m came from external grants and contributions. The remaining \u00a34.95m borrowing need was funded through the council's own cash reserves \u2014 a practice known as internal borrowing that saved \u00a372,488 in interest costs. The council held \u00a312.1m in total external debt at year-end. However, deploying reserves this way left the council with less cash to invest: its investment portfolio fell from an average of \u00a317.5m during the year to \u00a39.4m by the end of March, costing \u00a374,676 in foregone investment income. The council remained within its statutory borrowing limits, with all borrowing confined to capital spending. A separate service investment \u2014 a loan to Your Housing Limited \u2014 generated \u00a3307,000 in interest income as budgeted.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council (SMDC)","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","headline":"No breach of statutory borrowing limits; all borrowing confined to capital purposes","detail":"The Executive Director and Chief Finance Officer confirm that borrowing in 2025/26 was undertaken only for capital purposes and the statutory borrowing limit (the authorised limit) was not breached.","category":"governance","matters":"Compliance with prudential limits is a key indicator of financial control and governance; absence of any breach signals no financial stress or loss of market access","figures":"No figures","quote":"The Executive Director & Chief Finance Officer confirms that borrowing was only undertaken for capital purposes, and the statutory borrowing limit (the authorised limit) was not breached.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council (SMDC)","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","headline":"Service investment in housing loan to Your Housing Limited performing on budget at \u00a3307,000 annual interest income","detail":"The council has made a capital loan to Your Housing Limited (a service investment) which generated \u00a3307,000 in interest income during 2025/26, matching the budgeted return.","category":"income","matters":"Shows the council has income exposure to another entity through lending; performance is tracking expectations but lenders need to understand the counterparty risk","figures":"Interest income \u00a3307,000","quote":"\u00a3307,000 interest income was earned from the capital loan (service investment - housing) to Your Housing Limited in line with the budget.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council (SMDC)","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","headline":"Investment portfolio contracted from average \u00a317.5m to year-end \u00a39.4m due to internal borrowing strategy, causing \u00a374,676 income shortfall","detail":"The average investment portfolio during 2025/26 was \u00a317.5m across six institutions, yielding \u00a3667,354 in income. However, year-end investments fell to \u00a39.4m. The council missed its investment income target by \u00a374,676 because cash reserves were deployed as internal borrowing to reduce external debt service costs.","category":"income","matters":"The trade-off between lower borrowing costs and reduced investment income affects the council's total financial returns; it also indicates how much cash is available for this strategy","figures":"Average daily investments \u00a317.5m; year-end investments \u00a39.4m; investment income \u00a3667,354; income shortfall \u00a374,676; average return 3.81%","quote":"The average daily investment portfolio during 2025/26 was \u00a317.5million invested with six institutions, yielding \u00a3667,354 in investment income. There was a \u00a374,676 shortfall against the investment income budget due to a smaller investment portfolio than anticipated as a result of internal borrowing.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council (SMDC)","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","headline":"Total external debt of \u00a312.1m at year-end with \u00a372,488 underspend on borrowing costs through internal borrowing","detail":"The council holds \u00a312.1m in total external debt as of 31 March 2026. Using internal borrowing practices, the council spent \u00a372,488 less than budget on borrowing costs, showing an active strategy to minimize external debt service.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The actual debt position and the council's use of internal cash reserves to manage borrowing costs both affect the council's financial flexibility and future refinancing needs","figures":"Total external debt \u00a312.1m; borrowing cost underspend \u00a372,488","quote":"Total external debt at 31 March 2026 was \u00a312.1million. There was an underspend of \u00a372,488 against borrowing costs due to internal borrowing practices.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council (SMDC)","paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","headline":"Capital expenditure of \u00a317.1m created a borrowing need of \u00a34.95m after external funding of \u00a312.1m","detail":"The council spent \u00a317.1m on capital in 2025/26. After accounting for \u00a312.1m in external contributions, this generated a net borrowing requirement of \u00a34.95m. This shows the scale of capital investment and the portion that must be funded through borrowing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Lenders need to understand the council's capital investment trajectory, funding sources, and annual borrowing requirements to assess sustainability","figures":"Capital expenditure \u00a317.1m; external contributions \u00a312.1m; borrowing need \u00a34.95m","quote":"Capital expenditure in 2025/26 totalled \u00a317.1million. After external contributions of \u00a312.1million, this has created a borrowing need of \u00a34.95million.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"High Peak","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"No breach of statutory borrowing limits; all borrowing confined to capital purposes","lead":"Compliance with prudential limits is a key indicator of financial control and governance; absence of any breach signals no financial stress or loss of market access","also":["4 other findings in this window"],"watch":null,"n_findings":5,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Annual Treasury Management Report 2025/26, 31/07/2026 Audit &amp; Accounts Committee","top_paper_url":"https://highpeak.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s54281/54281.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":38,"news_drafts_total":1}}